     EXPLANATION OF OPTION
 
     Use this option to fail a drive through software.
 
     NOTE:  A procedure for failing a drive is included in the 
            help screens following the explanation of option.
 
 
     WHAT "FAIL" MEANS:  Failing a drive through software causes the 
     array controller to change the drive status from "Optimal" (0) or 
     "Warning" (5) to "Failed" (3).  Once a drive is failed, the array 
     controller can NOT access that drive's data until you replace the 
     drive and restore the logical unit.
 
     IMPORTANT NOTE:  Do NOT fail a drive in a RAID 0 logical unit
                      because there is no parity.  Do NOT fail a 
                      drive in a RAID 1, 3, or 5 logical unit if
                      the logical unit is degraded.
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     WHEN TO FAIL A DRIVE:  Fail a drive in the following 
     circumstances:
 
          - If the drive status is "Warning" (5) and you want
            to replace the drive.  You should fail the drive
            before you replace it.
 
          - When you want to format a drive in a logical unit.
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                                (CONTINUED)
     PROCEDURE -- FAILING A DRIVE
 
     IMPORTANT NOTE:  Before you fail a drive, back-up any
                      data on the logical unit file-by-file.  
                      Remember, do NOT fail a drive in a RAID 0 
                      logical unit because there is no parity.  
                      Do NOT fail a drive in a RAID 1, 3, or 5 
                      logical unit if the logical unit is already 
                      degraded.
 
     1.   Select a drive by pressing the arrow keys.
 
     2.   Press "f" to fail the drive.
 
     3.   If necessary, press "c" to confirm that you want to continue
          with the operation you selected, or press "q" to quit.
